# UI Components and Motion

Use the canonical design system in `docs/ui/QUANTA_UI_SYSTEM.md`.

## Design tokens

Define and consume semantic tokens for backgrounds, surfaces, text, borders, accent, status colors, spacing, radius, elevation, typography, motion and z-index. Do not scatter one-off visual constants through page-specific CSS.

Use an 8px-derived spacing rhythm and controlled radii. Quanta is dark-first but not pure-black or neon-first.

## Icons and typography

Use one consistent licensed SVG icon family. Outline-first, technical, geometric icons are preferred. Emoji are not primary UI icons.

Use a highly readable sans-serif UI font and a dedicated monospaced terminal/code font. Verify font licensing before bundling.

## Motion hierarchy

Animation must communicate hierarchy, continuity, state, progress or feedback. Remove animation that exists only for spectacle.

Default hierarchy:
- simple hover/focus/opacity/transform -> CSS transitions,
- moderate browser-native sequencing -> Web Animations API when appropriate,
- complex coordinated timelines -> evaluate GSAP only when CSS/WAAPI are insufficient.

GSAP is OPTIONAL, not a default dependency. If proposed, verify current license, maintenance, bundle impact and exact justified use before adding it.

Honor `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Terminal rendering

For a terminal surface, evaluate xterm.js before building a custom terminal renderer. Terminal behavior must sit behind a `TerminalAdapter` boundary so a mock implementation can later be replaced by a real gateway-backed implementation.

## Reuse

Prefer reusable Quanta primitives/composites for buttons, cards, status, progress, timer, toasts, skeletons, empty/error states, navigation, path/room cards, lab panels and terminal surfaces. Use implementation patterns natural to Jinja/Alpine/CTFd; do not imitate React component architecture unnecessarily.
